0+30+70+60=240. s<10: slot1 cap=20+s, slot3 cap=70 → total = 80+20+s+70+70 = 240+s → *0.5 = 120+0.5s >120. Interesting: because building load below? Wait site limit 120, building at slot3 50+s, cap = min(80, 70-s). For s<10, cap 70-s >60? Actually if s=0, cap=70; slot1 cap=20. Total = 80+20+70+70=240. s=5: slot1 25, slot3 65, total 80+25+70+65=240. Hmm I miscalculated: slot3 cap=70-s, not 70. Total = 80 + (20+s) + 70 + (70-s) = 240 constant. Right. So total capacity 120 kWh always for s in [0,40] (as long within bounds). Thus no extra total.\n\nBut per-bus capacity could shift between A and B? A window slots0-1. Increasing s increases slot1 cap, so A can get more than 60 kWh AC if s>10. B window slots2-3 decreases slot3 cap as s increases, but B needs 60. With s>10, B total cap slots2+3 = 70 + (70-s) = 140-s; at s=10, 130 → 65 kWh; at s=20, 120 →60. So at s=20 B exactly 60, A gets 80 + 40 = 120 → 60 kWh, exactly its need. Wait A's cap at s=20: slot0 80, slot1 40, total 120*0.5=60. So A exactly 60. For s between 10 and 20, A gets (80 + 20+s)=100+s kW → (50+0.5s) kWh; B gets 140-s kW → (70-0.5s) kWh. A needs 60, B needs 60. At s=20 both 60. At s<20, A<60, B>60. But A can only use its own window, cannot transfer B's surplus; and B cannot use more than needed (battery cap / required). So s must be ≥20 for A. At s=20 both exactly 60. For s>20, A>60 (surplus wasted) and B<60 (short). So s=20 is unique feasible. That matches handover. With actual measured building [40,80,60,60], the caps [80,40,60,60] yield A window 120 kW*0.5=60, B window 120 kW*0.5=60. Perfect balance. Interesting: actual building differs but still total per window exactly 120. So revised schedule works at 0.90 exactly.\n\nNow, question: could we revise building transfer to create margin? Actual measured loads differ. At actual energy meter, maybe the loads are [40,80,60,60] after transfer 20. If we reduce or increase transfer, maybe distribution changes. But given actual capture reflects the actual system, perhaps the transfer is fixed. The task asks \"离场余量\" — departure margin. With revised schedule, margins are 0. At η=0.90, no margin. At lower efficiency 0.85 infeasible, so release decision: do not dispatch both, perhaps decide to release neither / hold one? \"低效率时的放行决定\" — the handover should say at 85% do not release on original plan. The task says \"给出...低效率时的放行决定\" — give release decision at lower efficiency. Could be dispatchAt85 false. The shift-plan has field dispatchAt85 false. Should remain false. But maybe we should articulate a specific decision: hold both / reduce load. The handover currently says reduce task, extend window, or increase available power. It might be considered insufficiently decisive? The task wants \"放行决定\" (release decision). We need to make it explicit: at 85%, do not release either bus under this schedule; or perhaps release one? Let's analyze whether one can be released at 85%.\n\nAt η=0.85, each bus needs battery 54, AC 63.529. With total per-bus max AC 60, each short 3.529 kWh AC = 3 kWh battery exactly. Both buses independent windows. Can't release either fully to required 126/130. Could stage partial? Could one bus be released if we sacrifice the other? A's max AC is 60, B's max 60. Each cannot reach its own requirement regardless of the other because windows don't overlap, and total per window 60. So neither can be released at required levels at η=0.85. Unless we can shift building load differently to allocate more capacity to one window. With nominal loads, shifting s=10 gives A cap total 60? Let's compute A: slots0+1 = 80 + (20+s) = 100+s kW; over 0.5 h = 50+0.5s kWh. At s=10, 55; s=20, 60; s=40 (max), 70. B: slots2+3 = 70 + (70-s) = 140-s kW; over 0.5 = 70-0.5s. At s=10, 65; s=20, 60; s=40, 50. So at s=40, A gets 70 kWh AC (battery 59.5, enough for 54? yes 59.5 ≥54) and B gets 50 kWh AC (battery 42.5, short 11.5). So could release A only at 85% by shifting 40 kW (max) to give A enough, but B short. Or at s=10, B gets 65,